  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "L1 gold-context records report measured human absolute oral bioavailability values predominantly in the 50-100% range, all above the F>=20% threshold: doxazosin 62-75%, ACT-1004-1239 53%, suvorexant 82%, ofloxacin 80-95%, tegafur ~100%, nebivolol 96% in extensive metabolizers, balcinrenone 52%, dovitinib ~75%, mirtazapine ~50%, and baloxavir marboxil high.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C04","C05","C06","C08","C09","C10","C11","C12","C15","C17","C19","C21","C22"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 provides a direct oral-versus-IV comparison for tedizolid (oral AUC 26.1 vs IV 30.5 µg·h/mL; oral Cmax 2.25 vs IV 3.02 µg/mL), indicating high oral bioavailability for that reference, and L4 adds favorable solubility/dissolution evidence for polar analogs, including fexuprazan freely soluble at pH 4.0, PNU96391 with >90% GI absorption in rats, and verubecestat as BCS class 1 with very rapid dissolution.",
      "card_ids": ["C89","C107","C70","C80","C116","C147","C146","C159"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records for structurally related sulfonyl-azole/basic-amine/difluorophenyl molecules show low-to-moderate human hepatic intrinsic clearance: vonoprazan 2.73 µL/min/mg, pyrazole 5 9.1 mL/min/kg and <6 µL/min/million hepatocytes, larotrectinib 14.19 µL/min/mg, and BMS-690514 predicted moderate 8.3 mL/min/kg with low/moderate in vivo clearance; this does not indicate a strong hepatic first-pass liability.",
      "card_ids": ["C248","C242","C246","C252","C244","C245","C236"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Some L5/L6 records show P-gp/BCRP efflux and higher HLM CLint in less similar analogs (sulfonamide-difluorophenyl imidazoles 27-30 µL/min/mg; RO5263397 35 µL/min/mg), but none provides a direct F<20% outcome for the query, and efflux can coexist with F>=20% (ofloxacin 80-95%).",
      "card_ids": ["C196","C202","C188","C213","C179","C215","C182","C212","C166","C235","C259","C231","C224","C254","C19"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No affirmative threshold-relevant evidence supports F<20% for the query; the physicochemical permeability liability is generic and L5/L6 mechanism records are analog-based, not direct query outcomes.",
      "card_ids": []
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C248","C242","C246","C252","C244","C245","C236","C229","C223","C240"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct human or preclinical PK/bioavailability measurement for the query molecule.",
    "No query-specific oral AUC, Cmax, or IV-comparator data.",
    "No query-specific permeability, efflux, transporter, intestinal-metabolism, hepatic-clearance, or metabolic-stability assay; L6 records are for related but not identical molecules.",
    "No dose, formulation, or species context for the query.",
    "L6 record transfer likelihoods are moderate (~0.50-0.56), limiting direct extrapolation.",
    "No ChEMBL evidence available."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "High prediction retained and modestly strengthened. L1 gold-context records predominantly support F>=20%; L3/L4 add oral-exposure and solubility support; L6 hepatic-clearance records for closest sulfonyl-azole/difluorophenyl/basic-amine analogs show low-to-moderate human hepatic intrinsic clearance, reducing first-pass concern. Efflux and some higher-CLint analog records are not direct and do not establish F<20%. Confidence remains moderate."
}

